<!-- ================================================================ -->
<!-- 2. DATA SOURCES AND CANONICAL REPRESENTATIONS ≈ 900-1200 words.  -->
<!-- ================================================================ -->

<section xml:id="sec-data-canonical">
  <title>Data Sources and Canonical Representations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The framework separates environment-specific decoding from the analytic
      core.  PreTeXt source is converted into a canonical item specification,
      and Runestone's DataShop-formatted rows are converted into canonical
      events.  Reconstruction, validation, scoring, and feature construction
      operate on those canonical objects.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-pretext-items">
    <title>PreTeXt-Authored Card-to-Zone Items</title>

    <p>
      A sample <tag>cardsort</tag> item (<xref ref="fig-cardsort-rendered"/>
      left) is organized by <tag>match</tag> blocks defined in the <pretext/> source 
      (<xref ref="cardsort-source"/>). Each <tag>premise</tag> defines a draggable card, 
      and the <tag>response</tag> in the same block defines the slot to which those cards 
      belong. A <tag>premise</tag> in a <tag>match</tag> with no <tag>response</tag> 
      defines a decoy card that is not meant to be placed in any slot. A <tag>response</tag> 
      in a match with no <tag>premise</tag> defines a decoy slot that should remain empty.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cardsort-rendered">
      <caption>
        Initial Drag-and-Drop board (left) and key solution (right).
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="49% 47.5%">
        <image source="figures/rendered-cardsort.png" width="100%"/>
        <image source="figures/rendered-cardsort-soln.png" width="100%"/>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Source order is operationally important because Runestone identifies
      cards and slots using positional labels of the form
      <c>item_drag#</c> and <c>item_drop#</c>; the tray is labeled
      <c>dragzone</c>.  Decoding those labels back to authored objects
      therefore requires the corresponding item source and version.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="cardsort-source">
      <title>
        Authored <pretext/> <tag>cardsort</tag> source. The <c>Pentagon</c> is a decoy slot 
        since its <tag>match</tag> lacks a <tag>premise</tag>, and the <c>circle</c> is a decoy 
        card since it has no corresponding <tag>response</tag>.
      </title>
      <program line-numbers="yes">
        <![CDATA[
        <exercise>
          <statement> 
            Match each shape to its general category
          </statement>
          <cardsort>
            <match> 
              <premise>  equilateral triangle </premise>
              <premise>  right triangle       </premise>
              <response> Triangle             </response>
            </match>
            <match>
              <premise>  square               </premise>
              <response> Quadrilateral        </response>
            </match>
            <match>
              <response> Pentagon             </response>
            </match>
            <match>
              <premise>  circle               </premise>
            </match>
          </cardsort>
        </exercise>
        ]]>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      For item <m>q</m>, source order induces card indices
      <md>
        \cardset_q=\{1,\ldots,P_q\}
      </md>
      and destination-slot indices <m>\{1,\ldots,R_q\}</m>. The tray is zone <m>0</m>, 
      so the complete zone index set is 
      <md>
        \zoneset_q=\{0,1,\ldots,R_q\}
      </md>.
      The source defines the key configuration
      <md>
        s_q^\star:\cardset_q\rightarrow\zoneset_q
      </md>,
      where <m>s_q^\star(j)</m> is the home zone of card <m>j</m>.  It also supplies
      authored card and zone content, decoy structure, feedback-policy metadata, and 
      a source fingerprint used to detect item revisions.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-runestone-export">
    <title>Runestone DataShop-Formatted Event Logs</title>

    <p>
      Runestone Academy exports the interaction data as tab-delimited files
      organized according to a DataShop-style schema.  Each row records one
      event.  The fields used here include <c>Time</c>, <c>Anon Student Id</c>, 
      <c>Session Id</c>, <c>Problem Name</c>, <c>Feedback Classification</c>, 
      <c>Problem View</c>, <c>Selection</c>, <c>Action</c>, <c>Feedback Text</c>,
      and <c>Input</c>, together with course-location metadata.
    </p>

    <p>
      For the current cardsort logs, <c>Selection</c> distinguishes
      <c>dragNdrop-drop</c> move rows, <c>dragNdrop</c> check rows,
      <c>dragNdrop-reset</c> reset rows, and <c>page</c> rows.  Move payloads
      identify one positional card label and a destination label.  A check
      row's <c>Input</c> field contains the complete submitted configuration
      and is the authoritative source for anchor comparison; the corresponding
      <c>Action</c> value may be truncated in the export.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-input-contracts">
      <title>Platform-specific inputs and canonical outputs.</title>
      <tabular valign="top">
        <col width="18%" />
        <col width="50%" />
        <col width="32%" />
        <row header="yes">
          <cell><p>Input</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Adapter responsibility</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Canonical output</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>PreTeXt item source</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Index cards and zones, construct the key, identify decoys, and fingerprint the source.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Item specification</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Runestone <c>.tab</c> row</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Preserve identity fields and payloads, identify event type, and retain raw-row provenance.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Canonical event record</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <!--
    INPUT-DESCRIPTION COMPLETION CHECKLIST
