    2. Record the complete column header and compare it across every input file.
    3. Confirm whether any semester used materially different Selection or payload conventions.
    4. Produce a machine-readable item-source inventory with question ID, source path,
        source commit, source hash, P, R, key, decoy cards, and decoy zones.
    5. Verify positional card and zone labels against at least one raw move and one check per item.
    6. Add a short version table if source or logging conventions changed across offerings.
    7. Decide whether the full field-mapping table belongs in the supplement rather than the main text.
    -->
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-canonical-objects">
    <title>Canonical Item and Event Objects</title>

    <p>
      The canonical item specification contains an item identifier, card set,
      zone set, initial configuration, key configuration, authored labels,
      decoy indicators, feedback-policy metadata, and a source fingerprint.
      The canonical event record contains learner and item identifiers, an
      event timestamp, stable ordering fields, an event type, a decoded payload,
      and provenance linking the event to the original import and row.
    </p>

    <p>
      This boundary is what permits a platform-neutral analytic core.  Another
      authoring or delivery environment could use the same reconstruction
      procedure by supplying adapters that satisfy these contracts.  The
      present study validates only the PreTeXt and Runestone adapters.
    </p>
    
    <figure xml:id="fig-adapter-architecture">
      <caption>
        Platform adapters and the canonical reconstruction core. Two
        platform-specific inputs, PreTeXt <c>cardsort</c> source and
        Runestone's DataShop-formatted rows, pass through an item adapter and
        an event adapter into the canonical item specification and canonical
        event stream.  The platform-neutral core consumes only these
        contracts, performing replay reconstruction, anchor validation, QC and
        readiness assignment, and feature construction, and emits versioned
        event-, configuration-, attempt-, and item-level tables.  A new
        environment requires only new adapters that satisfy the same
        contracts.
      </caption>
      <image source="figures/fig-adapter-architecture.png" width="100%">
        <description>
          <p>
            A left-to-right flow diagram.  A dashed platform-specific region
            contains two branches: PreTeXt item source flowing into an item
            adapter that indexes cards and zones, constructs the key, identifies
            decoys, and hashes the source; and a Runestone export flowing into
            an event adapter that preserves identity and order fields,
            classifies event types, decodes payloads, and retains row
            provenance.  The branches produce the canonical item specification
            (cards, zones, key, decoys, authored labels, feedback policy, source
            hash) and the canonical event stream (identity, order, type, decoded
            payload, provenance), which converge on a platform-neutral core of
            four stacked stages: replay reconstruction, anchor validation, QC
            and readiness, and feature construction.  The core emits versioned
            outputs: event-, configuration-, attempt-, and item-level tables.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <!--
    ADAPTER-FIGURE CHECKLIST
